1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:07,972
Three thousand three hundred years ago, a teenage king was wrapped for eternity with a dagger on his right thigh.

2
00:00:07,972 --> 00:00:12,366
Its blade was iron, in an age when iron was worth more than gold.

3
00:00:12,833 --> 00:00:18,294
In two thousand sixteen, scientists confirmed what the metal really is.

4
00:00:18,294 --> 00:00:19,756
It came from space.

5
00:00:19,756 --> 00:00:24,833
The iron in Tutankhamun's dagger fell to Earth inside a meteorite.

6
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,408
Online, that was all it took.

7
00:00:27,408 --> 00:00:29,298
A dagger from outer space.

8
00:00:29,298 --> 00:00:31,189
Metal no human could make.

9
00:00:31,189 --> 00:00:33,006
A blade that never rusts.

10
00:00:33,006 --> 00:00:35,333
Maybe even a gift from visitors.

11
00:00:35,799 --> 00:00:38,304
So tonight, the dagger is our witness.

12
00:00:38,304 --> 00:00:42,062
We sit it down under the lamp and ask it seven questions.

13
00:00:42,062 --> 00:00:44,633
Every answer has to come with evidence.

14
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:50,553
At the end, we read back its statement, score every claim, and deliver a verdict.

15
00:00:50,553 --> 00:00:51,899
The tape is running.

16
00:00:56,366 --> 00:00:58,787
Question one: where were you found?

17
00:00:58,787 --> 00:01:00,793
November nineteen twenty-two.

18
00:01:00,793 --> 00:01:05,705
Howard Carter opens the tomb of Tutankhamun in the Valley of the Kings.

19
00:01:05,705 --> 00:01:09,233
But this witness stays hidden for three more years.

20
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:15,124
In nineteen twenty-five, the team unwraps the king's mummy, layer by layer.

21
00:01:15,124 --> 00:01:17,872
Among the linen they find two daggers.

22
00:01:17,872 --> 00:01:21,200
One lies on the abdomen, with a blade of gold.

23
00:01:21,666 --> 00:01:24,383
The other lies along the right thigh.

24
00:01:24,383 --> 00:01:30,182
Its hilt is gold too, but its blade is grey, polished, and almost free of rust.

25
00:01:30,182 --> 00:01:33,633
Carter catalogued it as object two fifty-six k.

26
00:01:34,099 --> 00:01:47,900
The hilt is a jeweller's masterpiece: bands of lapis lazuli, carnelian and malachite, diamond and wave patterns made of gold grains half a millimetre wide, and a pommel of rock crystal.

27
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:55,655
Its gold sheath carries a lily pattern on one side, feathers on the other, and ends in the head of a jackal.

28
00:01:55,655 --> 00:01:59,366
The whole dagger is about thirty-five centimetres long.

29
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:01,357
And it was not alone.

30
00:02:01,357 --> 00:02:08,977
The tomb held more iron: sixteen miniature blades, a tiny headrest, and an iron eye amulet on a bracelet.

31
00:02:08,977 --> 00:02:12,533
Iron objects, in a tomb from before the Iron Age.

32
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,281
That is the first oddity, and it is real.

33
00:02:16,281 --> 00:02:20,363
The Iron Age only begins around twelve hundred BCE.

34
00:02:20,363 --> 00:02:23,965
Tutankhamun died more than a century earlier.

35
00:02:23,965 --> 00:02:27,166
So where did a Bronze Age king get iron?

36
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:30,097
Question two: what are you made of?

37
00:02:30,097 --> 00:02:32,702
For decades, the answer was disputed.

38
00:02:32,702 --> 00:02:40,166
A study in nineteen ninety-four found only about three percent nickel and concluded the iron was ordinary.

39
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:51,659
In twenty sixteen, an Italian and Egyptian team led by Daniela Comelli used portable X-ray fluorescence, calibrated on real meteorites.

40
00:02:51,659 --> 00:02:52,966
Nothing was cut.

41
00:02:52,966 --> 00:02:54,599
Nothing was scraped.

42
00:02:55,066 --> 00:03:01,379
The answer: iron, with almost eleven percent nickel and about point six percent cobalt.

43
00:03:01,379 --> 00:03:04,500
That is the signature of an iron meteorite.

44
00:03:04,966 --> 00:03:06,636
Why does nickel matter?

45
00:03:06,636 --> 00:03:11,427
Iron meteorites carry between five and thirty-five percent nickel.

46
00:03:11,427 --> 00:03:18,106
Iron smelted from Earth's ores before the nineteenth century almost never tops four percent.

47
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:20,066
Eleven is not a close call.

48
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:28,702
Even the cobalt matches: seventy-six iron meteorites with similar nickel average point five seven percent cobalt.

49
00:03:28,702 --> 00:03:32,099
The blade sits right in the middle of the pack.

50
00:03:32,566 --> 00:03:35,652
Then a second team checked independently.

51
00:03:35,652 --> 00:03:42,803
In twenty twenty, Japanese researchers led by Takafumi Matsui scanned the whole blade in Cairo.

52
00:03:42,803 --> 00:03:45,813
They found nearly twelve percent nickel.

53
00:03:45,813 --> 00:03:46,566
Confirmed.

54
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:48,662
And it fits a pattern.

55
00:03:48,662 --> 00:03:57,176
Egypt's oldest iron, beads from Gerzeh, about five thousand two hundred years old, are hammered meteorite iron too.

56
00:03:57,176 --> 00:04:02,433
So is every Bronze Age iron object tested in a twenty seventeen survey.

57
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:06,662
So the alien claim gets its one true sentence here.

58
00:04:06,662 --> 00:04:09,317
Yes, this metal is extraterrestrial.

59
00:04:09,317 --> 00:04:11,088
But hold on to the word.

60
00:04:11,088 --> 00:04:12,711
It describes the rock.

61
00:04:12,711 --> 00:04:15,366
It says nothing yet about the hands.

62
00:04:15,833 --> 00:04:18,596
Question three: where did you fall from?

63
00:04:18,596 --> 00:04:28,199
Iron meteorites are pieces of the metal cores of small worlds that broke apart early in the solar system, more than four billion years ago.

64
00:04:28,666 --> 00:04:31,802
Some of those fragments cross Earth's orbit.

65
00:04:31,802 --> 00:04:32,872
Most burn away.

66
00:04:32,872 --> 00:04:40,000
A few chunks of iron survive the fall and land, heavy and black, in places like the Egyptian desert.

67
00:04:40,466 --> 00:04:44,885
Comelli's team searched the meteorite records for the region.

68
00:04:44,885 --> 00:04:47,348
Twenty iron meteorites were known.

69
00:04:47,348 --> 00:04:56,766
Only one came within ten percent of the blade's nickel and cobalt: a one-kilo stone called Kharga, found in the year two thousand.

70
00:04:57,233 --> 00:05:01,237
Kharga was found west of Alexandria, near Mersa Matruh.

71
00:05:01,237 --> 00:05:03,276
A suspect, not a conviction.

72
00:05:03,276 --> 00:05:10,266
Many meteorites fell and were never catalogued, and one match in chemistry is not a fingerprint.

73
00:05:10,733 --> 00:05:13,394
Matsui's scans added a second clue.

74
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:19,780
Mapping the nickel revealed faint bands crossing at angles, about a millimetre wide.

75
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:28,066
That is the Widmanstätten pattern, which forms only as metal cools over millions of years inside an asteroid.

76
00:05:28,533 --> 00:05:35,981
The bands told them the type of meteorite: an octahedrite, the most common kind of iron meteorite.

77
00:05:35,981 --> 00:05:37,121
Nothing exotic.

78
00:05:37,121 --> 00:05:39,933
Just the ordinary kind of space iron.

79
00:05:40,399 --> 00:05:50,866
So the witness's story so far: born in a shattered asteroid, fallen into a desert, picked up by someone who knew this black, heavy stone was something special.

80
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:53,808
Question four: how were you made?

81
00:05:53,808 --> 00:05:57,858
This is where the alien theory makes its boldest move.

82
00:05:57,858 --> 00:06:01,458
Iron basically did not exist, documentaries ask.

83
00:06:01,458 --> 00:06:03,858
This metal was not mined by man.

84
00:06:03,858 --> 00:06:05,133
So who forged it?

85
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:11,720
Those lines are real, quoted from an Ancient Aliens episode and another documentary.

86
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:14,343
And taken alone, they are not false.

87
00:06:14,343 --> 00:06:20,100
The trick, as historian Candida Moss pointed out, is in blurring two questions.

88
00:06:20,566 --> 00:06:22,578
Where did the metal come from?

89
00:06:22,578 --> 00:06:22,980
Space.

90
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:24,321
Who made the dagger?

91
00:06:24,321 --> 00:06:26,199
That is a separate question.

92
00:06:26,199 --> 00:06:29,954
A meteorite is raw material, like gold dug from a river.

93
00:06:29,954 --> 00:06:31,966
Someone still has to shape it.

94
00:06:32,433 --> 00:06:34,416
And the blade tells us how.

95
00:06:34,416 --> 00:06:41,319
Matsui's team listed the options: cold cutting, melting and casting, or heating and hammering.

96
00:06:41,319 --> 00:06:44,110
Casting would erase the crystal bands.

97
00:06:44,110 --> 00:06:45,800
But the bands survived.

98
00:06:46,266 --> 00:06:52,297
The dark, bubbly spots are the remains of troilite, an iron sulphide found in meteorites.

99
00:06:52,297 --> 00:06:57,379
They lost most of their sulphur, which takes roughly seven hundred degrees.

100
00:06:57,379 --> 00:07:02,800
But they never melted into the metal, which happens near nine hundred and fifty.

101
00:07:03,266 --> 00:07:09,552
So the smith worked in a window: hot enough to soften the iron, never hot enough to melt it.

102
00:07:09,552 --> 00:07:10,987
Heat, hammer, repeat.

103
00:07:10,987 --> 00:07:15,633
Low-temperature forging, below about nine hundred and fifty degrees.

104
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:19,273
We know that works, because people have done it.

105
00:07:19,273 --> 00:07:28,199
Japanese smiths forged a sword from the Gibeon meteorite, and it kept the same crystal pattern and the same black cracks from troilite.

106
00:07:28,666 --> 00:07:29,350
No laser.

107
00:07:29,350 --> 00:07:30,869
No impossible alloy.

108
00:07:30,869 --> 00:07:37,857
A skilled human smith, hammering stubborn meteorite iron into a blade two millimetres thick.

109
00:07:37,857 --> 00:07:42,566
Comelli called it evidence of early, successful iron smithing.

110
00:07:43,033 --> 00:07:45,575
Question five: why haven't you rusted?

111
00:07:45,575 --> 00:07:52,800
This is the claim that sounds most like magic: thirty-three centuries in a tomb, and the blade still shines.

112
00:07:53,266 --> 00:07:54,614
First, look closer.

113
00:07:54,614 --> 00:07:55,961
It is not spotless.

114
00:07:55,961 --> 00:08:03,052
There is a winding crack about five centimetres long, and dark spots along the edges and the middle.

115
00:08:03,052 --> 00:08:07,733
Those spots contain chlorine: a classic product of iron corrosion.

116
00:08:08,199 --> 00:08:13,036
Matsui compared it with Harry Burton's photo from nineteen twenty-five.

117
00:08:13,036 --> 00:08:15,624
The dark spots sit in the same places.

118
00:08:15,624 --> 00:08:22,300
The blade has not got worse since discovery, but it was already marked when it went into the tomb.

119
00:08:22,766 --> 00:08:24,106
Second, chemistry.

120
00:08:24,106 --> 00:08:30,361
Iron with around eleven percent nickel resists corrosion far better than plain iron.

121
00:08:30,361 --> 00:08:34,457
Metallurgists use nickel today for exactly that reason.

122
00:08:34,457 --> 00:08:41,233
Compare the ancient dagger from Alaca Höyük in Turkey, also meteoritic, and badly corroded.

123
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:42,925
Third, location.

124
00:08:42,925 --> 00:08:52,575
Tutankhamun's tomb is in one of the driest places on Earth, and the blade spent its time sealed in resins, linen and darkness.

125
00:08:52,575 --> 00:08:54,719
Rust needs water and oxygen.

126
00:08:54,719 --> 00:08:57,399
Here, it got very little of either.

127
00:08:57,866 --> 00:09:02,763
Nickel-rich metal, a dry sealed tomb, and a few rust spots after all.

128
00:09:02,763 --> 00:09:06,666
The miracle blade turns out to be a very well kept one.

129
00:09:07,133 --> 00:09:11,784
Our witness has now explained its origin, its metal and its shine.

130
00:09:11,784 --> 00:09:14,180
The next two questions are harder.

131
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:17,000
Even the experts still argue about them.

132
00:09:17,466 --> 00:09:19,375
Question six: who sent you?

133
00:09:19,375 --> 00:09:29,133
In the eighteen eighties, villagers at Amarna found the diplomatic archive of the pharaohs: hundreds of clay letters written in cuneiform.

134
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:38,448
Among them are gift lists from Tushratta, king of Mitanni, a powerful kingdom in what is now Syria and southern Turkey.

135
00:09:38,448 --> 00:09:46,033
He was sending his daughter Taduhepa to marry Amenhotep the Third, probably Tutankhamun's grandfather.

136
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:55,743
And in letter twenty-two, among the treasures, the scribes list a dagger whose blade is of iron, its guard of gold, with designs.

137
00:09:55,743 --> 00:09:59,899
A few lines later, another iron dagger, with lapis lazuli.

138
00:10:00,366 --> 00:10:07,842
Iron-bladed daggers, sent as royal presents to Tutankhamun's family, a generation before he was buried.

139
00:10:07,842 --> 00:10:12,633
That is the strongest written clue to where our witness came from.

140
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:16,014
Matsui's team found a chemical clue too.

141
00:10:16,014 --> 00:10:20,459
The gold hilt contains a few percent calcium without sulphur.

142
00:10:20,459 --> 00:10:30,733
They read that as lime plaster used to glue the decorations, a material common in Mitanni but not used in Egypt until a thousand years later.

143
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:33,815
But this is where the experts disagree.

144
00:10:33,815 --> 00:10:41,998
A published reply argued the calcium may be a measurement artefact, since it also shows up on the blade and other objects.

145
00:10:41,998 --> 00:10:46,156
And none of the letter's descriptions fits our dagger exactly.

146
00:10:46,156 --> 00:10:47,766
One has a haft of ebony.

147
00:10:48,233 --> 00:10:57,607
The team behind the book Iron from Tutankhamun's Tomb notes the pommel resembles Aegean work, while the dagger type is Egyptian.

148
00:10:57,607 --> 00:11:03,100
Their conclusion: a reliable answer on who made it is currently impossible.

149
00:11:03,566 --> 00:11:09,200
So the honest answer to question six is a shrug with evidence on both sides.

150
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:11,275
A Mitanni gift is plausible.

151
00:11:11,275 --> 00:11:14,018
Egyptian craftsmen are not ruled out.

152
00:11:14,018 --> 00:11:18,465
But notice who is on the list of suspects: kings and smiths.

153
00:11:18,465 --> 00:11:19,799
Nobody from orbit.

154
00:11:20,266 --> 00:11:23,240
Question seven: why bury you with a king?

155
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:28,100
Because in the fourteenth century BCE, iron was not a weapon metal.

156
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:34,266
It was a treasure, rarer than gold and reserved for ritual, ornament and royal gifts.

157
00:11:34,733 --> 00:11:38,206
Did the Egyptians know it fell from the sky?

158
00:11:38,206 --> 00:11:47,205
A generation or so after Tutankhamun, a new Egyptian word for iron appears, usually translated as iron of the sky.

159
00:11:47,205 --> 00:11:50,600
The Hittites spoke of black iron of heaven.

160
00:11:51,066 --> 00:12:00,067
The small iron blades from the tomb look like the tools of the Opening of the Mouth, the ritual that restored the dead king's senses.

161
00:12:00,067 --> 00:12:04,533
Ancient temple lists connect those tools with iron and with stars.

162
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:12,045
For a culture that pictured its kings joining the stars, a blade forged from star-iron was not a weapon.

163
00:12:12,045 --> 00:12:15,433
It was a statement about where the king was going.

164
00:12:15,899 --> 00:12:23,858
Placed on the right thigh, inside the wrappings, it was one of the most precious objects a pharaoh could take with him.

165
00:12:23,858 --> 00:12:24,928
Not a curiosity.

166
00:12:24,928 --> 00:12:26,466
A crown jewel of metal.

167
00:12:26,933 --> 00:12:29,865
And this idea was not unique to Egypt.

168
00:12:29,865 --> 00:12:37,811
From the Shang dynasty in China to the Inuit of Greenland, people across history worked meteorite iron.

169
00:12:37,811 --> 00:12:40,666
Space iron was humanity's first iron.

170
00:12:41,133 --> 00:12:42,259
Seven questions.

171
00:12:42,259 --> 00:12:43,244
Seven answers.

172
00:12:43,244 --> 00:12:46,200
Time to read back the witness's statement.

173
00:12:46,666 --> 00:12:47,462
The statement.

174
00:12:47,462 --> 00:12:49,566
Let's score what our witness told us.

175
00:12:50,033 --> 00:12:55,513
Meteoritic iron, with eleven percent nickel and cobalt to match: evidence.

176
00:12:55,513 --> 00:13:00,846
Crystal bands showing low-temperature forging by human smiths: evidence.

177
00:13:00,846 --> 00:13:04,771
Every tested Bronze Age iron is meteoritic: evidence.

178
00:13:04,771 --> 00:13:09,733
Iron daggers listed as royal gifts in the Amarna letters: evidence.

179
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:13,627
Alien makers or impossible metal: no evidence.

180
00:13:13,627 --> 00:13:18,843
The never-rusting blade: debunked, it has rust spots and good reasons.

181
00:13:18,843 --> 00:13:22,866
Who forged it, and which meteorite it was: still open.

182
00:13:23,333 --> 00:13:24,245
The verdict.

183
00:13:24,245 --> 00:13:28,272
Tutankhamun's dagger really is made from a meteorite.

184
00:13:28,272 --> 00:13:30,248
The metal came from space.

185
00:13:30,248 --> 00:13:31,691
The dagger did not.

186
00:13:31,691 --> 00:13:39,366
Human smiths, probably in the fourteenth century BCE, forged it at low heat into a royal masterpiece.

187
00:13:39,833 --> 00:13:49,966
What stays open is smaller and more human: whose workshop made it, whether it came from Mitanni as a wedding gift, and which desert stone it was cut from.

188
00:13:50,433 --> 00:13:55,906
Ancient people looked up, saw iron falling from the sky, and turned it into treasure.

189
00:13:55,906 --> 00:13:57,838
That is not proof of visitors.

190
00:13:57,838 --> 00:14:00,799
It is proof of how closely they were watching.

191
00:14:01,266 --> 00:14:03,326
Which answer surprised you most?

192
00:14:03,326 --> 00:14:08,733
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
